p.src:
The next contribution of lecture 147 is that it tightens what the book means by evidence. A founder may have a real problem, a team, and money already being spent, and still not yet have a business in the stronger sense. Abraham’s correction is sharp: $\text{substantial problem alone}
\;\not\Rightarrow\;
\text{great business}.$ What is missing is not seriousness of the pain. What is missing is a repeatable path to customers: $\text{great business idea}
\;\Rightarrow\;
\bigl(\text{substantial problem}\bigr)
\land
\bigl(\text{customer acquisition can scale}\bigr).$

